WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.370
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:09.160
Consider this problem: we have a pair of images
that are visually quite different, but have

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.970
similar semantic meanings, and we wish to
map points between them.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:16.299
Now this might sound a bit weird, so bear
with me for a moment.

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:21.340
For instance, geese and airplanes look quite
different, but both have wings, and front,

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.460
and back regions.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:27.210
The paw of a lion looks quite different from
a cat's foot, but they share the same function

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.000
and are semantically similar.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.160
This is an AI-based technique that is able
to find these corresponding points between

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:38.210
our pair of images, in fact, the point pairs
you've seen so far have been found by this

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.210
AI.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.370
The main difference between this and previous
non-learning-based techniques is that instead

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:48.570
of pairing up regions based on pixel color
similarities, it measures how similar they

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:52.850
are in terms of the neural network's internal
representation.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.560
This makes all the difference.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.440
So far this is pretty cool, but is that it?

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.600
Mapping points?

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:03.710
Well if we can map points effectively, we
can map regions as a collection of points.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:06.329
This enables two killer applications.

00:01:06.329 --> 00:01:11.899
One, this can augment already existing artistic
tools so that we can create a hybrid between

00:01:11.899 --> 00:01:13.010
two images.

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:17.180
And the cool thing is that we don't even need
to have any drawing skills because we only

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:22.710
have to add these colored masks and the algorithm
finds and stitches together the corresponding

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.549
images.

00:01:25.549 --> 00:01:29.549
And two, it can also perform cross-domain
image morphing.

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:32.479
That's an amazing term, but what does this
mean?

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:36.350
This means that we have our pair of images
from earlier, and we are not interested in

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:40.749
stitching together a new image from their
parts, but we want an animation where the

00:01:40.749 --> 00:01:46.029
starting point is one image, the ending point
is the other, and we get a smooth and meaningful

00:01:46.029 --> 00:01:47.439
transition between the two.

00:01:47.439 --> 00:01:50.250
There are some really cool use-cases for this.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:55.490
For example, we can start out from a cartoon
drawing, set our photo as an endpoint, and

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:58.270
witness this beautiful morphing between the
two.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:03.209
Kind of like in style transfer, but we have
more fine-grained control over the output.

00:02:03.209 --> 00:02:04.209
Really cool.

00:02:04.209 --> 00:02:08.200
And note that many images inbetween are usable
as-is.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.039
No artistic skills needed.

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:14.290
And of course, there is a mandatory animation
that makes a cat from a dog.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:18.700
As usual, there are lots of comparisons to
other similar techniques in the paper.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:23.290
This tool is going to be invaluable for, I
was about to say artists, but this doesn't

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:28.819
require any technical expertise, just good
taste and a little bit of imagination.

00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:30.769
What an incredible time to be alive.

00:02:30.769 --> 00:02:34.449
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

